# Live Unity state through Hera

When a task depends on the running Unity Editor, first run this from the project root:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\tools\hera\capture-unity-context.ps1
```

Then read:

- `artifacts/hera-cursor/context.md`
- `artifacts/hera-cursor/status.txt`
- `artifacts/hera-cursor/scene.json`
- `artifacts/hera-cursor/console-errors.json`
- `artifacts/hera-cursor/scene_view.png` and `game_view.png` when visual state matters

For focused follow-up checks, Cursor may run the Hera CLI directly:

```powershell
hera-agent-unity status
hera-agent-unity scene info --compact-json
hera-agent-unity console --type error --compact-json
hera-agent-unity list --compact
```

The files are snapshots, not a live stream. Re-run the capture script after code, scene, or Inspector changes. If Hera reports no Unity instances, do not guess the live state; ask for Unity to be opened with the Connector package.

Use Hera for Unity inspection, Editor changes, Play Mode checks, screenshots, and Console verification. Keep Google Drive, Notion, and Jira work in their respective connectors; Hera does not replace those integrations.

Do not run `hera-agent-unity update` for this project. Treat deletion, broad reserialization, arbitrary C# execution, and package changes as high-impact actions requiring explicit confirmation.
